just think back to the enormous  Powerball fervor that gripped the entire  country that's okay for me to develop a  lineup and enjoy the aspect of becoming  a general manager for a team for a day  that's not okay  Brian Greenwood is a professor husband  and father he also represents the  typical fantasy sports player unlike  traditional fantasy leagues that span  over the course of an entire season  daily fantasy contests last a single day  in 2015 over 56 million Americans  played fantasy sports since the first  sight showed up online nearly 10 years  ago daily fantasy has grown to a  multi-billion dollar business with  draftkings and FanDuel leading the  industry I'm a casual or micro stakes  players what they say so I own a daily  basis is anywhere from five to ten  dollars a day is what out would I'll  spend in entry fees I really like the  excitement of trying to get that line up  perfect where you get that big payday  now I haven't had that big payday yet  while Greenwood waits for his big payday  many states are examining the legality  of one-day fantasy sports leagues that  have exploded in popularity over the  last few years just tuned your  television to any sports channel and  you're likely to see ads for a daily  fantasy sports contest it's like a new  season every time you play and best of  all you could win a shipload of money  while sports betting is mostly illegal  in the u.s. fantasy sports has a special  exemption thanks to the unlawful  internet gambling Enforcement Act passed  by Congress in 2006 but even with the  federal carve out the legal status of  daily fantasy remains murky because each  state gets to define what constitutes  gambling mark Edelman a law professor at  Baruch College and writer for Forbes  magazine has covered the daily fantasy  industry since its infancy under state  law a contest and tete is considered to  be illegal gambling if three elements  are met consideration which is an entry  fee prize and chance while that standard  is the same in all 50 states what  differs state-by-state  the definition of chance despite  questions of its legality fantasy sports  sites operated mostly under the radar  until an incident in the fall of 2015  brought scrutiny to the industry out of  the scandal swirling around those  fantasy sports sites New York's Attorney  General now investigating FanDuel and  draftkings to find out if some of their  employees one large amounts of money  thanks to inside information the story  that's told pretty much by the media is  that a draftkings employee had won a  high-stakes contest on FanDuel with  purported access to insider information  about who people were predicting while  Edelman supports some measure of  regulation to prevent fraud he sees  other factors contributing to daily  fantasy investigations but there are two  other pieces to the puzzle that led to  much greater attention for daily fantasy  sports in New York the daily fantasy  sports companies until just about a year  ago spent very little money on  television advertising they were not  seen as big business and not getting  much attention well in week 1 of the NFL  season this year both draftkings and  FanDuel finished on the top 10 list for  most money spent on television  advertising so activities that were  operating almost under the radar perhaps  seeing me too small for government  attention now was seen as big business  at the same time daily fantasy ads  returning heads New Jersey had their  effort to legalize sports betting  quashed by the u.s. Third Circuit Court  of Appeals  perhaps frustrated by the fact sports  leagues were making money off daily  fantasy and not supporting sports  gambling in his state representative  Frank pallone penned a letter to his  colleagues in Congress warning of the  dangers of daily fantasy this is this is  unregulated it's the Wild West you don't  really know based on what we found out  last week that you're making a fair bet  pallone found hypocritical the fact that  Major League Baseball and the NBA were  directly profiting from daily fantasy  sports but wanted to keep out sports  gambling and rather than write the  letter in the tone that talked about the  virtues of allowing sports gambling in  New Jersey pallone coined this letter  warning of the risks of daily fantasy  sports and suggesting it needs to be  investigated with growing government  attention to daily fantasy activities  and  now news of a possible scandal new  york's ambitious Attorney General Eric  Schneiderman decided to go after  operations in his state arguing they  were illegal games of chance daily  fantasy sports which we've been looking  into for over a month we've concluded is  not some new version of fantasy sports  it's really just a new version of online  gambling in his cease and desist notices  sent to draftkings and FanDuel  Schneiderman claimed the company's  engaged in illegal activity as they  promote daily fantasy sports like a  lottery representing the game to New  Yorkers as a path to easy riches that  anyone can win and here's where things  get confusing because in New York it's  legal to play the lottery in fact their  sales pitch is surprisingly similar to  that of daily fantasy sites introducing  sweet million from the New York Lottery  your best chance to win a million for a  dollar and it's not just the lottery  New Yorkers can also bet on horse racing  or visit any of the six licensed Indian  casinos in the state and these state run  gambling institutions are often prone to  this same corruption that occurs in  private sector operations so why are  those forms of gaming ok and not daily  fantasy sports  UNLV director of Gaming research David  Schwartz explains I think to an extent  recent state actions are about  protecting innocent victims and ensuring  clear protection but another big piece  of that has to be then protecting their  monopoly to regulate and offer gambling  services within their state the debate  around fantasy sports isn't so much an  argument over the definitions of skill  versus chance but evidence that  America's pseudo ban on sports gambling  is inherently hypocritical and  ineffective since the early days there's  been two different competing feelings in  America one is that people like to  gamble so let them do it  the other one is well everybody except  for me can't really handle gambling so  to me this is just the latest wrinkle as  long as technology advances and this is  going way back to the first dice you  know as long as technology is advancing  people are going to use it to gamble the  fantasy sports industry isn't going down  without a fight  and is quickly mobilizing in response to  Schneiderman's crusade Steve Miller who  played part in the effort to legalize  online poker has organized the daily  fantasy sports Players Alliance to  represent players across the country  no one wants to play a game where the  rules of the game change state-by-state  players want to play a game and we just  want the protection we know that we're  playing in a even playing field even  Dallas Mavericks owner and business  mogul Mark Cuban has thrown in his  support speaking before the fantasy  sports trade association this January  when the [ __ ] hits the fan right it  tends to blow all the [ __ ] everywhere  but then it's clean so while today  really seems bad and while it seems like  you're under attack that's the good news  because when they're attacking you  everything has to be cleaned up the  proverbial crap may be hitting the fan  for fantasy sports but will it provide  an opportunity to an act scene or gaming  laws I really wish we were like the rest  of the world where sports gambling was  legal and we could get it out of the  shadows my thing is when things are in  the shadows the potential for abuse is  far greater I think what it's really  important to remember at the end of the  day is that people have been gambling  for a really long time probably for more  than 10,000 years there's been balls  against it there's in laws legalizing it  the nice thing to do might be to have a  rational conversation about to what  extent does our society want adults who  don't have problems gambling to be  allowed to gamble prohibition doesn't  work it didn't work in poker it didn't  work with alcohol it doesn't work with a  host of other ranges of Industry  prohibition with DFS is not the answer

